WEBVTT

00:00:00.499 --> 00:00:02.233
NARRATOR: Horses frightened the Arawaks

00:00:02.233 --> 00:00:04.733
when they first saw them,
for they believed them

00:00:04.733 --> 00:00:08.799
to be giant dogs that fed on human flesh.

00:00:08.799 --> 00:00:12.033
But over time, Native Americans
came to use the horse

00:00:12.033 --> 00:00:13.699
for their own purposes.

00:00:13.699 --> 00:00:16.467
These dancers of the North American plains

00:00:16.467 --> 00:00:17.931
lived by hunting buffalo.

00:00:17.932 --> 00:00:23.633
But stalking the buffalo on foot
was a slow and dangerous affair.

00:00:23.633 --> 00:00:26.200
Horses revolutionized the hunt.

00:00:26.200 --> 00:00:29.532
What once took days now took moments.

00:00:29.532 --> 00:00:33.166
But in time, the buffalo of
the plains were replaced

00:00:33.167 --> 00:00:34.300
by European cattle.

00:00:34.300 --> 00:00:39.232
With them, a new culture
evolved, that of the cowboy.

00:00:39.233 --> 00:00:44.133
The all-American rodeo is
actually anything but.

00:00:44.133 --> 00:00:50.699
The bucking bronco, the branded
cattle, even the word "rodeo"

00:00:50.699 --> 00:00:53.600
are foreigners, all Spanish imports.
